                <h2>Case Opening: Burning $500 in Three Days Without a Single Winner</h2>
<p>When a tool app team was doing a TikTok Shop cold launch in Southeast Asia, operations lead Xiao Lin ran into this dilemma:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They uploaded 12 initial creatives covering product demos, user pain point scenarios, and limited-time offers. With a $50 budget per creative, the account balance hit zero after three days, but the cost per acquisition (CPA) remained stuck at $8-12, with no creative delivering positive ROAS.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Problem Diagnosis:</strong> Heavy creative homogenization, testing cycle too short, and equal budget distribution across all creatives.</p>
<p>This scenario is typical during TikTok Shop cold launches. This article reconstructs the team's adjustment process and extracts a directly applicable creative pool building SOP.</p>
<h2>1. The Underlying Logic: Tiering Isn't About Quality, It's About Phases</h2>
<p>Many people misunderstand "creative tiering" as putting "good creatives in Group A, bad ones in Group B."</p>
<p><strong>The correct tiering logic divides by testing phase:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tier</th>
<th>Name</th>
<th>Per-Creative Budget</th>
<th>Survival Period</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>L1</td>
<td>Exploration</td>
<td>$20-30</td>
<td>24 hours</td>
<td>Rapid elimination of ineffective directions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>L2</td>
<td>Validation</td>
<td>$50-80</td>
<td>48-72 hours</td>
<td>Confirm click-through and conversion paths</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>L3</td>
<td>Scale</td>
<td>$100+</td>
<td>Continuous monitoring</td>
<td>Expand exposure of winning creatives</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>During cold launch, L1 tier is the主力 (main force). Prepare 15-20 L1 creatives of different directions and run all data before making decisions.</p>
<h2>2. Four Key Metrics for Judging Creative Viability</h2>
<p>Not all metrics need constant monitoring. During cold launch, focus on these four:</p>
<p><strong>1. 3-Second Retention Rate</strong><br />
TikTok's algorithm prioritizes whether the first 3 seconds retain users. Creatives below 25% should be eliminated immediately.</p>
<p><strong>2. Click-Through Rate (CTR)</strong><br />
CTR below 1.5% means the opening hook has failed. No matter how good the rest of the content is, it won't perform.</p>
<p><strong>3. Cost Per Acquisition (CPA)</strong><br />
Calculate CPA after 10-15 add-to-carts. Stop investing in creatives exceeding target CPA by 2x.</p>
<p><strong>4. Creative Lifecycle</strong><br />
If a creative shows flat data for 3 consecutive days, it has entered decline and needs fresh creatives to take over.</p>
<h2>3. Cold Launch Creative Direction Allocation</h2>
<p>Returning to the opening case, the team's adjusted creative allocation:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Product Feature Demos</strong>: 3 creatives, showing core feature operation flow</li>
<li><strong>User Pain Point Scenarios</strong>: 5 creatives, covering different usage situations</li>
<li><strong>Before/After Comparisons</strong>: 2 creatives, emphasizing pre/post differences</li>
<li><strong>Social Proof/Review Style</strong>: 2 creatives, creating third-party endorsement feel</li>
<li><strong>Pure Promotion</strong>: 3 creatives, limited-time offers driving immediate decisions</li>
</ul>
<p>15 L1 creatives across 5 directions, $25 per creative, total investment $375.</p>
<p><strong>Result:</strong> After 72 hours, 2 creatives exceeded 2% CTR entered L2 tier; 1 creative with 40% retention rate directly entered L3 for scaling.</p>
<h2>4. Creative Iteration Cadence: Better to Pause Than Force</h2>
<p>Another mistake teams make: when creative performance is poor, they choose to "wait longer."</p>
<p><strong>Standard Actions:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>L1 creatives not reaching CTR ≥ 1% within 24 hours → Pause</li>
<li>L2 creatives exceeding target CPA by 150% within 48 hours → Pause</li>
<li>Daily data review, keep Top 20% creative directions, eliminate 80%</li>
</ol>
<p>The essence of cold launch isn't "nurturing creatives" but "rapid elimination, rapid iteration." Allocating budget to proven creatives is more efficient than casting a wide net.</p>
<h2>5. An Easily Overlooked Point: Creative-Landing Page Alignment</h2>
<p>Many teams only focus on ad metrics, ignoring the post-click landing experience.</p>
<p><strong>Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Does the landing page feature corresponding selling points for what the creative showcases?</li>
<li>Is the path shortest for the action the creative guides (add-to-cart/checkout)?</li>
<li>Are the offers promised in the creative clearly reflected on the landing page?</li>
</ul>
<p>One team once ran a creative with 3.5% CTR but extremely low conversion. Later they discovered the creative showcased "Premium features" while the landing page defaulted to the free version entrance—users couldn't find the paid upgrade path and bounced immediately.</p>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>The essence of TikTok Shop cold launch is finding scalable creative directions at minimum cost. A creative pool isn't about quantity but clear tiering, decisive elimination, and efficient iteration.</p>
<p>If you're in the cold launch phase, start with "15 creatives, 5 directions, $375" in small-budget testing, then scale based on data.</p>
